autogen.sh: stop after generating, when NOCONFIGURE is set

The script ends with `./configure $@', which is the right default for a
developer running it by hand and wrong for every cross build: this tree is
configured afterwards, with a --host, a --build, a sysroot and a pkg-config
that all have to be on that command line.  Run the way autogen.sh runs it --
with no arguments at all -- configure either fails outright ("cannot run C
compiled programs", because it is handed the cross compiler and no --host to
explain it) or, worse, succeeds against the BUILD machine's own GTK and leaves
a tree configured for the wrong system.

NOCONFIGURE is the convention for "generate the build system and stop", and is
what this port's configure driver sets before calling any autogen.sh.  Most
projects honour it; this one predates it.

--- a/autogen.sh
+++ b/autogen.sh
@@ -6,5 +6,7 @@
   && intltoolize --force --copy --automake \
   && autoheader \
   && automake --add-missing --gnu --copy \
-  && autoconf \
-  && ./configure $@
+  && autoconf
+
+# NOCONFIGURE is the convention for "generate the build system and stop".
+test -n "$NOCONFIGURE" || ./configure $@
